From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Greg Daniel <egdaniel@google.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 14:16:57 -0400
Subject: Validate direct-mask Slug data during deserialization

Direct-mask glyphs are packed into the glyph atlas without padding when
atlas bilerp is unsupported, so they are only safe to draw at integer
device positions, with an integer translation of their creation matrix.
Slug deserialization did not enforce this: a crafted Slug could supply
fractional or non-finite glyph positions, or a direct-mask creation
matrix that differs from the Slug's initial position matrix (bypassing
SlugImpl::canDraw), leading to out-of-bounds atlas sampling under MSAA.
Unbounded offsets could also overflow the float-to-int conversion used
when clipping direct-mask quads.

Deserialization now requires:
* direct-mask glyph positions to be finite integers within +/-2^21;
* direct-mask creation matrices to equal the Slug's initial position
  matrix, and canDrawDirect to match the SubRun type;
* finite bounds, origins, matrices, scales and SDFT ranges, and
  invertible, non-perspective creation matrices;
* all glyph positions to be finite and within 1e14;
* boolean flags to be 0 or 1 (read with readBool; the wire format is
  unchanged).

At draw time, VertexFiller::CanUseDirect also requires the offset
between the creation matrix and the position matrix to be a finite
integer within +/-2^21. The position matrix is computed exactly as at
creation (single-precision preTranslate), so drawing with an unchanged
matrix always yields a zero offset. SlugImpl::canDraw now only gates
Slugs that contain direct-mask SubRuns; every other SubRun type is
padded in the atlas (entries are keyed by padding) or doesn't use it.

Creation and deserialization share the same checks, so every Slug that
is created deserializes. SlugImpl::Make returns null if the position
matrix overflows or the bounds or origin are invalid. In
SubRunContainer::MakeInAlloc, which is used by all GPU text, not just
Slugs:
* direct masks are only used if the position matrix is finite,
  invertible, and has a translation within 1e14. Otherwise the glyphs
  fall back to drawables or paths;
* direct-mask glyphs whose device position exceeds 2^21 fall back to
  the drawable/path/transformed-mask stages;
* SDFT and transformed-mask SubRuns are only created if their creation
  matrix (and SDFT matrix range) is valid. For example, an infinite
  fGlyphsAsPathsFontSize gives large text an infinite SDFT range, so
  that text now uses direct masks or paths instead;
* glyphs with positions beyond 1e14 are rejected.

